## Goal
See how the six samples (A1-A3 untreated, B1-B3 treated) cluster: a principal component analysis of the gene-level counts, with samples coloured by condition.

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve through each sub-stage's OUTPUTS.tsv at execution time; the paths
below are what they resolve to today.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| counts_gene (native) | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper | run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv |
| design | 01_samplesheets | 01_samplesheets/rnaseq_bulk_design.csv (`sample_id,condition,group,replicate`) |

## Method
Environment: `gars-bio` (pandas, numpy, scikit-learn, matplotlib). All steps run in one script, `scripts/sample_pca.py`. Inputs are read-only.

1. Read the counts matrix (columns `gene_id`, `gene_name`, then one column per sample) and the design table; check that the sample columns and design `sample_id` values match exactly, else fail.
2. Drop genes with zero counts in all samples.
3. Transform: log2(count + 1).
4. Keep the 500 genes with the highest variance across the six samples (all genes if fewer than 500 remain).
5. Centre each gene to mean zero; no per-gene scaling.
6. PCA with `sklearn.decomposition.PCA(n_components=min(6, n_samples - 1), svd_solver="full")`; write per-sample coordinates and the explained-variance fraction of each component.
7. Plot PC1 vs PC2, one point per sample labelled with its sample ID, coloured by `condition` (two colours, with legend); axis labels carry the percent variance explained. Save as PNG.

